Let $a(n,k)$ represent the total number of elements across all integer partitions of $n$ where no part appears with a frequency of at least $k$. If expressed as a sum, this quantity is given by $a(n,k)=\sum_{\lambda \in P(n,k)} |\lambda|$. This paper establishes several identities concerning $a(n,k)$ and provides two distinct combinatorial proofs for each. Furthermore, we demonstrate that $a(n,k)$ satisfies the recurrence relation:
\begin{align*}
a(n,k)&=a(n-1,k)+a(n-k,k)+\sum_{j=1}^{k-1}a(n-(k+1)-j^2,k).
\end{align*}
Finally, assuming a fixed modulus $m$, we derive formulas that calculate $a(n,k) \pmod m$.

