We introduce a new method for solving equations of the form
\begin{subequations}\label{eq:main}\begin{align}
  \partial_t u &= \nabla \cdot (a(x) \nabla u) + f(u), & x &\in \Omega,~ t>0,\\
  u &= g, & x &\in \Omega_0,
\end{aligned}\end{subequations}
where $a(x)$ is a symmetric, bounded, and measurable positive definite matrix, and $\Omega_0$ is a subset of $\Omega$. Our proposed novel approach constructs a sequence of approximations $u^k$ that converges to the solution $u$ of the system. Each $u^k$ is a weak solution to a specific parabolic variational inequality. A key advantage is that the coefficient $a(x)$ remains unchanged, unlike many existing methods for which we provide theoretical convergence results under broad conditions. Our numerical experiments demonstrate that the parabolic initial-boundary value problem for the coarsening of grained materials can be modeled using a specific choice of $f(u)$ and $\Omega_0$.
